messages:
sam: FYI I'm seeing a couple of customers hit TS/typing errors + runtime failures when they try to use the JS SDK on edge runtimes (Cloudflare/Denoland/Next Edge) with ESM bundles. Repro attached in a small sandbox.

lee: ugh, this is the classic undici vs global fetch mess. Edge runtimes provide fetch but types/lib mismatch and our published bundles don't gracefully fall back. Any repro notes?

sam: repro steps: 1) create an ESM project (type: "module") 2) import our sdk `import { Redwood } from 'redwood-inference'` 3) run on Cloudflare Worker — immediately fails with "fetch is not defined" (even though worker has fetch), and TS shows RequestInit/Response not found in compiled types.

priya: a few things colliding: package exports (esm/cjs), the presence/absence of lib.dom types at compile time, and runtime detection. Cloudflare/deno/proxy runtimes use a slightly different global shape. Also some bundlers strip types.

jason: concrete suggestions: (A) publish dual ESM/CJS with proper exports (import vs require), (B) ship a tiny runtime wrapper that picks the right fetch, (C) include a small .d.ts augmentation for server builds so TS users don't get missing DOM types.

isa: can you paste the tiny wrapper you have in mind? That's the thing we can add to the SDK quickly and ship as a patch.

priya: sure, here's a minimal pattern we've been using (works without top-level await):

```ts
// src/runtime/fetch-pick.ts
let _fetch: typeof globalThis.fetch | undefined;
export async function getFetch() {
  if (_fetch) return _fetch;
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch !== 'undefined') {
    _fetch = globalThis.fetch;
    return _fetch;
  }
  // dynamic import avoids failing in ESM/edge where undici isn't available
  const undici = await import('undici');
  _fetch = undici.fetch;
  return _fetch;
}

export function fetchWrapper(...args: Parameters<typeof globalThis.fetch>) {
  // caller must await getFetch() in init path
  throw new Error('fetch not initialized');
}
```

lee: and then in your SDK surface we do something like:
```ts
// during SDK init
const runtimeFetch = await getFetch();
export const apiCall = (url, opts) => runtimeFetch(url, opts);
```

sam: ok but the typing problem still bites TS-only users who don't want lib.dom in tsconfig. Any pattern to ship types safely?

priya: add a small declaration file we publish alongside the package. Example: `src/types/fetch-shim.d.ts`

```ts
// fetch-shim.d.ts
export {};
declare global {
  function fetch(input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit): Promise<Response>;
}
```

Then include it from your package.json types entry: "types": "dist/index.d.ts" and ensure the declaration gets emitted. This avoids forcing consumers to enable lib.dom.

jason: also we should update package.json exports so bundlers pick the right entry: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.esm.js",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs.js"
  }
}
```

isa: does anyone worry about coupling to undici for node-hosted workloads? Some customers expect our SDK to use node's built-in fetch (node18+).

lee: runtime detection covers that: prefer globalThis.fetch when available. For performance/reliability on Node we can recommend undici in docs, but not bundle it as the only option. Also callsite-level polyfill: `if (!globalThis.fetch) globalThis.fetch = (await import('undici')).fetch;` is fine in Node environments.

sam: what about ESM/CJS import problems e.g. users doing `const { Redwood } = require('redwood-inference')` and failing because package is ESM only?

priya: publish both bundles (we already do some of this) and add a tiny compatibility note in the README. For quick wins: add a compatibility test to CI matrix: run node (cjs/esm), deno, and a Cloudflare Worker smoke test against a mocked endpoint.

jason: action items I can take: 1) add the runtime fetch wrapper to the SDK repo, 2) add the .d.ts fetch shim and ensure our type build includes it, 3) update exports field and add tests.

isa: I'll update the onboarding sample app to demonstrate the two patterns: server-side Node (polyfill undici if needed) and edge (no polyfill). That should cut down onboarding friction.

typebot: automated note: a ticket has been created -> RDW-7624 (sdk: fetch runtime/typing compatibility).

sam: thanks all. One more weird gotcha: Next.js edge functions sometimes bundle away our dynamic import line and still fail at runtime. If we see that in tests, we should add a tiny synchronous fallback that throws a useful message and points at docs.

lee: agreed. I'll add a diagnostics error with actionable message: "No runtime fetch available — see https://redwood.example/sdk/fetch-polyfill for required polyfill steps." :thumbsup:

priya: leaving a small snippet for docs that dev-rel can copy into a quickstart: 
```bash
# Node server (add to top of app entry)
if (!globalThis.fetch) {
  import('undici').then(m => { globalThis.fetch = m.fetch; });
}
```

sam: perfect. For the thread: let's roll this into a small 0.1.1 patch and add CI checks. I'll follow up with the PR link.

isa: ping me when the docs PR is up, I'll wire it into the onboarding checklist.

sam: thanks everyone :rocket:
